# Public GitHub release

When the user asks to finish public-repo polish, do the following:

## Sanitize the repo
- Remove personal or private information (emails, local paths, machine-specific notes, private keys, keystores, tokens, `.env*`, credentials under `/credentials/`)
- Confirm `.gitignore` covers `credentials/`, keystores, `.env*`, local SDK paths, and build artifacts
- Do not commit the Android signing keystore or Expo/EAS secrets

## README
- Create or update a clear `README.md` covering: what the app is, features, how to run with Expo, Android APK build, iOS build notes, language-pair usage, and how GitHub Actions releases work

## Versioning and releases
- Follow `.cursor/rules/versioning.mdc`: ask before any version bump
- Prefer GitHub Actions workflow `.github/workflows/release-apk.yml` (**Release Android APK**) to bump, build `Canal-Study.apk`, and publish a GitHub Release
- Release tags must be semver (`v1.2.0`); APK asset name must remain `Canal-Study.apk` for in-app updates
- `expo.extra.updates.githubOwner` / `githubRepo` must match this repository
- When running the workflow, fill the optional **notes** input with short user-facing release notes; GitHub also appends auto-generated notes from commits
- Commit messages must follow `.cursor/rules/commit-messages.mdc` so auto release notes stay readable
- **Never change** `expo.android.package` (`com.canalstudy.app`) or the SQLite file name (`canal-study.db`) — that is what keeps user cards across APK updates. Schema changes must migrate in place, not wipe tables.
